-/* =============================================================================================================
- * BGALAZKA'S EXTENSION TO THE ZENTRAL MOD (v2)
- * -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- * ARCHITECTURAL NOTES & INSTRUCTIONS FOR FUTURE AI AGENTS:
- * 0. DEFAULT-OFF CONTRACT (CRITICAL): extension CSS must be visually inert on a fresh install. Any selector that
- *    changes normal browser/Zentral UI must be gated by an explicit `bgalazka-*="true"` opt-in attribute. Keep
- *    extension-settings styling scoped to #zs-panel-bgalazka so installing the extension does not restyle base
- *    Zentral settings. New feature CSS must follow the same opt-in rule.
- * 1. ONLY modify or append code BELOW this marker when working on Bgalazka's Extension.
- * 2. ALWAYS deliver fully compiled, complete ready-to-paste chunks for both the JS and CSS extension sections.
- * 3. EXPLAIN THE "WHY" IN CODE COMMENTS: Document Gecko/Zen workarounds so future models do not undo working solutions.
- * 4. TAB CRASH GUARD: NEVER attach a MutationObserver with { childList: true, subtree: true } to the tabstrip,
- *    the settings modal's ancestor tree, or documentElement. Modifying/observing tab children during
- *    gBrowser.pinTab() triggers a fatal C++ assertion crash in Gecko's frame constructor. This includes
- *    observers that merely WATCH an ancestor of the tabstrip with subtree:true, even if they don't mutate
- *    anything themselves — the previous version of this file did exactly that to detect the settings modal
- *    opening (observing documentElement), which is the same forbidden pattern and the most likely cause of
- *    "crashes when pinning". Detect UI state changes via method hooks instead (see section 5).
- * 5. SCOPE BUG (CRITICAL): `ZentralApps`, `ZentralTabGroups`, and `ZentralSettings` are `class` declarations
- *    scoped INSIDE the base mod's own top-level IIFE. They are NOT global identifiers. Referencing the bare
- *    class name from this separately-appended IIFE (`typeof ZentralApps !== "undefined"`) always evaluates to
- *    false, silently skipping any code guarded by it — this is what happened in the previous version and is
- *    why the opposite-docking/pin-state hooks never actually ran. Always reach the class via the singleton
- *    instance instead: `window.Zentral.Apps`, `window.Zentral.TabGroups`, `window.Zentral.Settings`.
- * 6. TAB CLICK PASS-THROUGH: Any click on a tile inside a tab bubbles to the tab unless mousedown, mouseup,
- *    click, and auxclick are stopped in the CAPTURE phase (e.stopPropagation()). This prevents essential tabs
- *    from switching on LMB.
- * 7. OPPOSITE-SIDE DOCKING MATH: Native Zentral's positionPanel() calculates targetRight as
- *    (window.innerWidth - sidebarRect.left). If the sidebar is on the left, targetRight evaluates to ~1920px,
- *    pushing the panel completely off the screen! We must hook positionPanel() and isPanelAttachedToRight()
- *    on the Apps INSTANCE (see note 5) to force root.style.right/left = "12px" directly.
- * 8. RESIZE INVERSION: Zentral's onDrag() internally checks this.isPanelAttachedToRight() ? (startW - diff) : (startW + diff).
- *    Hooking isPanelAttachedToRight() to return true when docked opposite makes native Zentral invert resize
- *    math automatically — no need to touch onDrag/startResize ourselves.
- * 9. PANEL OPACITY: Web pages inside <browser> render opaque backgrounds. Applying only background-color to
- *    the slider is invisible behind the page canvas. Real transparency requires setting CSS `opacity` on
- *    #zen-app-panel-slider itself.
- * 10. NATIVE FEATURE PARITY (upstream): the base mod now natively sets data-loaded="true"/"false" on tiles
- *     (in renderGrid/getOrCreateAppBrowser/closeApp, searched via a document-wide querySelectorAll so it stays
- *     correct even after we move a tile into an essential tab's corner) and natively handles middle-click
- *     unload on the tile itself (mousedown+auxclick, stopPropagation). We must NOT reimplement either of
- *     these anymore — our old duplicate implementations were overwriting the native, more-accurate state and
- *     silently blocking the native middle-click handler from ever running (capture-phase
- *     stopImmediatePropagation upstream prevents the tile's own listeners from firing at all).
- * 11. CORNER-TILE DOCKING STABILITY: assigning tiles to essential tabs by array INDEX reshuffles every
- *     existing assignment whenever a tab is added/removed/reordered, and Zen's split-view feature can clone a
- *     pinned tab (with whatever tile is docked inside it) producing a duplicate icon. Docking must be done by
- *     a STABLE ID persisted on the tab element itself, and only against tabs that are genuine members of
- *     gBrowser.tabs (not split-view clones).
- * 13. PILL CONTRAST: the native pill's background (`var(--zen-colors-tertiary, ...)`) is a THEME color that can
- *     land close to the page's own colors behind it, making the pill's icons hard to read. Expanded/hovered
- *     pills therefore use a plain BLACK background with forced white icons. Its alpha is controlled by the
- *     dedicated --bgalazka-pill-background-opacity variable, while --bgalazka-pill-peek-opacity controls ONLY
- *     the shrunk idle mini pill. The two settings are intentionally independent so a subtle idle marker does
- *     not make the opened pill controls equally transparent.
- * 14. WEB TOOLBAR SEARCH QUICK-SWITCH BUTTON: see Zentral_uc.js note 14 for the JS half (search-engine
- *     picker, URL-vs-search detection, Back-button home fallback). The quick-switch button reuses the exact
- *     same `.zen-toolbar-btn` sizing/hover styling as back/forward/reload below -- its visibility (only on a
- *     recognized DDG/Startpage results page) is toggled from JS via inline style.display rather than a CSS
- *     attribute selector, since it depends on the CURRENT PAGE'S URL, which has no CSS selector to key off.
- * 15. TOOLBAR TOP DOCKING (v4): see the "TOOLBAR POSITION: TOP-OF-PANEL" rules near the end of this file.
- *     Flipping `order` from 999 to a very negative number is enough to move the toolbar from last to first
- *     child of the #zen-app-panel-slider flex column -- no separate top-docked layout was needed. The
- *     autohide overlay case additionally needs its own top/bottom + transform-direction flip since that mode
- *     positions the toolbar with `position: absolute` instead of relying on flex order at all.
- * 16. ALL-SIDES RESIZE (v5): native Zentral only ever offers ONE resize edge (.zen-app-resize-strip, the
- *     side facing the browser content, width-only). We add two MORE strips, .zen-app-resize-strip-top and
- *     .zen-app-resize-strip-bottom, that let the user drag the panel's height too. These are the ONE part of
- *     this whole extension that needs NO per-side (data-panel-side) or opposite-docking CSS at all: the panel
- *     always spans the full width of #zen-app-panel-root regardless of which side it's docked on or whether
- *     dual-view/push is active, so a single unconditional rule set covers every mode. See "ALL-SIDES PANEL
- *     RESIZE" near the end of this file, and Zentral_uc.js note 16 for the JS half (drag math + persistence).
- * 17. ALL-SIDES RESIZE: WHY THE INNER (SIDEBAR-FACING) EDGE HAS NO STRIP (v5 follow-up): only 3 of the 4
- *     edges got a drag surface -- top, bottom, and the existing native outer (content-facing) edge. The 4th,
- *     the edge touching the sidebar side (e.g. the LEFT edge when data-panel-side="left"), was deliberately
- *     left alone. Reason: top/bottom are cheap because positionPanel() recomputes them from scratch on EVERY
- *     call, so layering a saved "extra" on top every time is naturally idempotent (note 16). Width has no such
- *     from-scratch recomputation -- root.style.width is native persisted STATE (panelWidthPx), only ever
- *     touched by onDrag/updateWidthVar/toggleExpand/opposite-docking's safe-max clamp/dual-view's push-margin
- *     sync (note 12). Dragging the inner edge would need to change width AND the anchor (left/right) together
- *     while keeping the outer edge fixed -- but with no idempotent "natural width" to layer on top of the way
- *     top/bottom have, we'd have to cache-and-diff against a value five other pieces of native/extension code
- *     also mutate, which is exactly the class of fragile, easy-to-silently-break interaction note 5's whole
- *     "reach the singleton, don't reimplement" philosophy exists to avoid. Left unimplemented on purpose; see
- *     Zentral_uc.js note 17 for the same reasoning from the JS side.
- * 18. PANEL POSITION DRAG / URL BAR GRIP (v6+): a small grip (.zen-toolbar-urlbar-drag-handle) inside the web
- *     toolbar's URL bar lets the user drag the WHOLE panel in 2D (reposition, not resize). Vertical movement
- *     uses the persisted top/bottom position offset and horizontal movement uses the persisted physical panel
- *     offset, so both axes restore exactly after temporary Dual-View / Edge-Attached suppression. See
- *     Zentral_uc.js note 18 for the drag math. `.zen-toolbar-urlwrap` had to gain `display: flex` to lay the grip and the `<input>` out side
- *     by side -- the pre-existing hide rule for it (right below) had to gain `!important` to match, since our
- *     new `!important` display declaration would otherwise always beat a plain one regardless of selector
- *     specificity, silently breaking the "hide URL bar" toggle.
- * 19. OPPOSITE-DOCKING STRIP/HOVER-ZONE ATE CLICKS MEANT FOR THE PAGE'S OWN SCROLLBAR (v6 fix): section 2's
- *     per-side .zen-app-resize-strip AND .zen-app-hover-zone overrides only ever changed which edge they sit
- *     on (left/right), never their offset-vs-width relationship, so both kept the native "straddle the edge"
- *     geometry (part outside the panel, part inside, on top of page content). For data-panel-side="left" that
- *     inward part lands on the panel's right/inner edge -- which is also where a page's own vertical
- *     scrollbar ALWAYS renders (LTR or RTL, Gecko paints it on the physical right) -- and both elements have
- *     pointer-events:auto while the panel is open, so they intercepted scrollbar clicks/drags before the
- *     <browser> ever saw them. The hover-zone was the bigger offender (44px wide native, ~20px of it inward at
- *     the old -24px offset, and it also drives the pill's :hover reveal -- hence hovering near that edge would
- *     visibly "activate" the pill even when nothing was deliberately being hovered) but the resize-strip
- *     contributed too (~6px inward at the old -8px offset). Fix, applied to BOTH elements on BOTH sides for
- *     consistency: offset changed to exactly -(element's own width) so each sits ENTIRELY outside the panel
- *     (0px inward overlap) instead of straddling the edge -- same interactive-area size as before, just moved
- *     outward. See the inline comments on all four affected rules in section 2 for the per-element math.
- * 20. NORMAL-MODE MINI PILL COVERED PAGE CONTENT WHEN IT GREW (v6.1 fix, superseding an earlier v6 attempt):
- *     section 3b originally reused native's own translate(-50%/50%) "straddle the edge" transform for the
- *     mini-pill/peek-dot feature, just scaled down when idle. A first pass fixed a resulting ~15px horizontal
- *     drift (caused by an incorrectly edge-anchored transform-origin fighting that straddle transform) by
- *     restoring the default center origin -- which fixed the drift, but exposed the real, underlying design
- *     problem: a straddle transform inherently sits HALF the pill on top of the panel/page even at rest, and
- *     growing it from a centered pivot expands that overlap equally in both directions, so part of the page
- *     became genuinely unreachable under the expanded pill. Opposite-docking's mini-pill (section 2) never had
